-- Q960: Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 172(a) and (b) as amended by the TCJA, NOLs arising after December 31, 2017 may be carried forward indefinitely but are limited to offsetting 80% of taxable income in any carryforward year. The TCJA also eliminated the two-year carryback for most taxpayers, with limited exceptions for farming losses under Section 172(b)(1)(B).
Wrong (B): The two-year carryback with 20-year carryforward and no income limitation was the pre-TCJA rule, which no longer applies to post-2017 NOLs.
Wrong (C): Post-TCJA NOLs do not expire but are subject to the 80% limitation rather than being unlimited.
Wrong (D): NOLs are not eliminated; they carry forward indefinitely under current law.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 960;

-- Q961: Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 461(l), noncorporate taxpayers may not deduct aggregate net business losses exceeding the threshold amount ($305,000 for single filers in 2026, indexed annually). The $445,000 excess ($750,000 minus $305,000) is recharacterized as an NOL carryforward under Section 172, subject to the 80% taxable income limitation in subsequent years.
Wrong (A): Excess business losses are not permanently disallowed; they are carried forward as an NOL to future tax years.
Wrong (B): Section 461(l) applies regardless of whether the taxpayer materially participates in the trade or business.
Wrong (C): The excess is treated as an NOL carryforward, not limited to offset against capital gains.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 961;

-- Q962: Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns [easy/L1]
